from lib import *
C=10;S='maths';CH='algebra'
lesson(C,S,CH,'gcd-lcm-and-rational-expressions',"GCD, LCM and rational expressions","Simplifying a recipe","A recipe for 12 people uses 6 eggs and 9 cups of flour. Dividing both by the largest common number, 3, gives the simpler ratio 2 : 3. Polynomials can be simplified in the same way.",
["The GCD of two polynomials is the polynomial of the highest degree that divides both exactly. @ GCD",
 "To find it, factorise each polynomial and multiply the common factors, taking the smallest power of each. @ common factors",
 "The LCM is the polynomial of the lowest degree divisible by both, found by taking every factor with its highest power. @ LCM",
 "As with numbers, GCD x LCM = product of the two polynomials (up to a constant). @ GCD x LCM",
 "A rational expression is a fraction p(x)/q(x) where p and q are polynomials and q is not zero. @ p(x)/q(x)",
 "To simplify it, factorise numerator and denominator and cancel the common factors. @ cancel factors",
 "To add rational expressions, take the LCM of the denominators; to multiply, multiply numerators and denominators; to divide, multiply by the reciprocal. @ LCM of denominators"],
("Simplify (x squared - 9)/(x squared + 5x + 6).",[("Factorise the top: x squared - 9 = (x - 3)(x + 3).","Difference of squares."),("Factorise the bottom: x squared + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3).","Find two numbers with sum 5 and product 6."),("Cancel (x + 3): the result is (x - 3)/(x + 2), where x is not -3 and not -2.","Never allow a zero denominator.")]),
("How do you find the GCD of two polynomials and simplify a rational expression?",["Factorise both","Multiply the common factors","Cancel common factors in a fraction"],["Cancelling terms instead of factors"]),
["GCD: product of common factors with smallest powers; LCM: all factors with highest powers.","A rational expression is p(x)/q(x) with q not zero.","Simplify by factorising and cancelling common factors.","Cancel only factors, never single terms."],
["m|GCD of x squared - 1 and x squared + x - 2 is|x - 1|x + 1~x squared - 1 = (x-1)(x+1) and x squared + x - 2 = (x-1)(x+2);x + 2~Not a factor of x squared - 1|Common factor is x - 1.",
 "t|In (x + 3)/(x + 5) we may cancel x to get 3/5.|False|We can cancel factors, not individual terms.",
 "m|x squared - 4 factorises as|(x-2)(x+2)|(x-4)(x+4)~Check by expanding;(x-2)(x-2)~That gives x squared - 4x + 4|It is a difference of squares.",
 "t|A rational expression can have denominator zero.|False|Division by zero is not defined.",
 "m|Simplify (x squared - 4)/(x - 2)|x + 2|x - 2~Factorise the numerator;x + 4~Not correct|(x-2)(x+2)/(x-2) = x + 2.",
 "n|LCM of x and x squared has degree|2|The LCM is x squared.",
 "t|GCD of two polynomials has degree at most that of each polynomial.|True|It divides both of them.",
 "s|The fraction p(x)/q(x) of two polynomials is a ____ expression.|rational|By definition."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'quadratic-equations-solving',"Quadratic equations: factorisation, completing the square and formula","The garden path","A rectangular garden is 3 m longer than it is wide, and its area is 28 square metres. What are its sides? Writing x(x + 3) = 28 gives a quadratic equation.",
["A quadratic equation is ax squared + bx + c = 0 with a not equal to 0. Its solutions are called roots. @ ax^2 + bx + c = 0",
 "Method 1, factorisation: split the middle term and use that if pq = 0 then p = 0 or q = 0. @ factorisation",
 "Method 2, completing the square: write x squared + (b/a)x as a perfect square plus a number, then take square roots. @ completing the square",
 "Method 3, formula: x = [-b plus or minus the square root of (b squared - 4ac)] divided by 2a. @ quadratic formula",
 "The expression b squared - 4ac is the discriminant, written delta. @ discriminant",
 "If delta is positive there are two different real roots; if delta = 0 there are two equal real roots; if delta is negative there are no real roots. @ nature of roots",
 "The sum of the roots is -b/a and the product of the roots is c/a. @ sum and product"],
("Solve x squared - 5x + 6 = 0, then use the formula on 2x squared + 3x - 2 = 0.",[("Factorise: x squared - 5x + 6 = (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3.","Numbers with sum -5 and product 6 are -2 and -3."),("For 2x squared + 3x - 2: a = 2, b = 3, c = -2, delta = 9 + 16 = 25.","Find delta first."),("x = (-3 plus or minus 5)/4, so x = 1/2 or x = -2.","Both values satisfy the equation.")]),
("List the three methods of solving a quadratic and what the discriminant tells us.",["Factorisation, completing the square, formula","Discriminant is b squared - 4ac","Positive, zero, negative give two, equal, no real roots"],["Forgetting the sign when b is negative"]),
["Quadratic: ax squared + bx + c = 0, a not zero.","Roots by factorisation, completing the square or the formula.","delta = b squared - 4ac decides the nature of the roots.","Sum of roots = -b/a, product = c/a."],
["m|The roots of x squared - 7x + 12 = 0 are|3 and 4|2 and 6~Product must be 12 and sum 7;-3 and -4~Sum would be -7|(x-3)(x-4) = 0.",
 "n|Discriminant of x squared + 4x + 4 is|0|16 - 16 = 0, so equal roots.",
 "t|If the discriminant is negative, the equation has no real roots.|True|The square root of a negative number is not real.",
 "n|Sum of the roots of x squared - 9x + 20 = 0 is|9|-b/a = 9.",
 "n|Product of the roots of 2x squared - 5x + 3 = 0 is|1.5|c/a = 3/2 = 1.5.",
 "m|The quadratic formula uses|-b plus or minus root(b squared - 4ac) over 2a|b over 2a~Missing the root part;-b over a~This is the sum of roots|It is the standard formula.",
 "t|x squared = 25 has two real roots.|True|x = 5 or x = -5.",
 "n|Roots of x squared - 3x = 0: the larger root is|3|x(x - 3) = 0, so x = 0 or 3."])
lesson(C,S,CH,'matrices-basics',"Matrices: order, types and operations","The school canteen","The canteen records how many idlis, vadas and dosas were sold on 3 days. Writing the numbers in rows and columns makes a compact table - a matrix.",
["A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of numbers in rows and columns, written in brackets. @ matrix",
 "A matrix with m rows and n columns has order m x n, read m by n. @ order m x n",
 "Special types: row matrix (one row), column matrix (one column), square matrix (m = n), zero matrix, diagonal, identity (1 on the main diagonal, 0 elsewhere). @ types of matrices",
 "Two matrices are equal if they have the same order and equal corresponding entries. @ equal matrices",
 "To add or subtract matrices they must have the same order; we add or subtract corresponding entries. @ add same order",
 "To multiply by a scalar k, multiply every entry by k. @ scalar multiplication",
 "To multiply matrix A (m x n) by B (n x p), the number of columns of A must equal the number of rows of B; the product is m x p. Each entry is row times column. @ AB defined"],
("If A = [[1, 2],[3, 4]] and B = [[0, 1],[5, 2]], find A + B and 2A.",[("A + B: add corresponding entries: [[1+0, 2+1],[3+5, 4+2]] = [[1, 3],[8, 6]].","Both are 2 x 2, so addition is allowed."),("2A: multiply every entry by 2: [[2, 4],[6, 8]].","Scalar multiplication."),("A + B = [[1,3],[8,6]] and 2A = [[2,4],[6,8]].","Order stays 2 x 2.")]),
("State when two matrices can be added and when they can be multiplied.",["Same order to add","Columns of A = rows of B to multiply","Product has order m x p"],["Multiplying entry by entry"]),
["Order m x n = m rows by n columns.","Add/subtract only matrices of the same order.","AB exists when columns of A = rows of B.","In general AB is not equal to BA."],
["m|The order of a matrix with 2 rows and 3 columns is|2 x 3|3 x 2~Rows come first;5~That is the total of both numbers|Order is rows x columns.",
 "t|A matrix with equal rows and columns is a square matrix.|True|m = n.",
 "n|The number of entries in a 3 x 4 matrix is|12|3 x 4.",
 "t|We can add a 2 x 2 matrix and a 2 x 3 matrix.|False|Their orders differ.",
 "n|If A = [[2, 3]] and k = 3, the sum of the entries of kA is|15|kA = [[6, 9]], and 6 + 9 = 15.",
 "m|If A is 2 x 3 and B is 3 x 2, AB has order|2 x 2|3 x 3~Take rows of A and columns of B;2 x 3~That is the order of A|m x p = 2 x 2.",
 "t|AB is always equal to BA.|False|Matrix multiplication is generally not commutative.",
 "s|A matrix with 1s on the main diagonal and 0s elsewhere is the ____ matrix.|identity|By definition."])
